A = {
"eu174050": dict(
 bluf="Frederick's seizure of Silesia launched Prussia as a great power and began a century-long rivalry with Austria for leadership of the German world. It showed that a small, militarized state could take territory by force and keep it.",
 why="Frederick saw a chance to grab a rich province while Maria Theresa's right to inherit the Habsburg lands was disputed.",
 trig="Emperor Charles VI died in October 1740, leaving his daughter Maria Theresa as heir.",
 then="The War of the Austrian Succession (1740-48) and the Seven Years' War confirmed Prussia's hold on Silesia, one of Austria's richest provinces. Maria Theresa responded by reforming Austria's state and army.",
 now="The Austro-Prussian rivalry ended only when Prussia unified Germany without Austria in 1866-71. Silesia became Polish in 1945, and its German population was expelled.",
 lens="Successions are moments of maximum vulnerability: opportunists strike when a new ruler's legitimacy is in doubt."),
"me-diriyah": dict(
 bluf="The pact between Muhammad ibn Abd al-Wahhab and Muhammad bin Saud fused a strict reformist religious movement with a dynasty's political ambitions. It is the founding bargain of the Saudi state: the Al Saud rule, and the Wahhabi clergy legitimize them.",
 why="Each side needed the other: the ruler gained religious legitimacy and a cause to expand, the preacher gained protection and power.",
 then="The first Saudi state conquered much of Arabia, including Mecca and Medina, before Ottoman-Egyptian forces destroyed it in 1818. The alliance was revived twice, finally under Ibn Saud in the 20th century.",
 now="The partnership remains the foundation of the Saudi state. Oil wealth later funded the spread of Wahhabi-influenced teaching worldwide; since 2017 Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman has curbed the clergy's power.",
 lens="Rulers who depend on a religious establishment for legitimacy must share power with it, until they find another source of legitimacy, such as prosperity."),
"na175415": dict(
 bluf="A frontier clash over the Ohio Valley escalated into the first truly global war between Britain and France. Britain's victory removed France from North America but left debts that led to the taxes that provoked the American Revolution.",
 why="Both Britain and France claimed the Ohio Valley, and the French built forts there to link Canada with Louisiana.",
 trig="Washington's attack on a French party at Jumonville Glen in May 1754.",
 then="Britain won, but its huge war debt led it to tax the colonies. Native nations lost their ability to play the two empires against each other.",
 now="Without this war there would have been no American Revolution a decade later.",
 lens="Skirmishes between great powers' local forces can escalate into world wars."),
"eu175551": dict(
 bluf="The Lisbon earthquake destroyed one of Europe's richest cities and shook Enlightenment confidence in a benevolent, orderly universe. It also produced the first state-led, rational reconstruction and an early scientific study of earthquakes.",
 why="A great earthquake on the sea floor off Portugal, followed by a tsunami and fires.",
 then="Voltaire's Candide mocked the optimism that 'all is for the best'. Portugal's chief minister Pombal rebuilt central Lisbon on a grid with earthquake-resistant timber frames and sent questionnaires to every parish about the quake, an early seismological survey.",
 now="It is often called the first modern disaster: studied scientifically, managed by the state and argued over by philosophers.",
 lens="Disasters test a society's beliefs and its state: watch who takes charge and which ideas lose credibility."),
"eu175652": dict(
 bluf="The Seven Years' War was the first global war, fought in Europe, North America, India, West Africa and on the oceans. Britain emerged as the dominant colonial and naval power, but the debts it ran up set off the American Revolution, while France's losses and debts pushed it toward its own revolution.",
 why="Colonial rivalry between Britain and France, and Austria's wish to win back Silesia from Prussia.",
 then="At the Peace of Paris (1763) France gave Canada and the land east of the Mississippi to Britain and Louisiana to Spain, and Britain took Florida from Spain. In India, French power was broken; in Europe, Prussia kept Silesia. Britain's national debt nearly doubled.",
 now="It began a century and a half of British global primacy and created the conditions for both the American and French revolutions.",
 lens="Winning a war for dominance can sow the next crisis: debt and overstretch follow victory."),
"as175724": dict(
 bluf="Plassey gave the East India Company control of Bengal, India's richest province, through bribery and betrayal more than battle. It turned a trading company into a territorial power and began nearly two centuries of British rule in India.",
 why="The Nawab resented the Company's fortifications and its abuse of trade privileges, and Clive plotted with discontented Bengali nobles and bankers.",
 trig="The Nawab's capture of Calcutta in 1756, and the deaths of British prisoners in the 'Black Hole', which the Company used to justify war.",
 then="The Company installed Mir Jafar as a puppet Nawab and extracted huge payments; after Buxar (1764) it won the right to collect Bengal's land revenue. Bengal's taxes then paid for further conquest, and Company misrule contributed to the devastating Bengal famine of 1770.",
 now="Plassey is often called the start of the British Empire in India, and Indian historians cite the 'drain' of Bengal's wealth as the start of colonial impoverishment.",
 lens="Empires are often built by exploiting local divisions: the decisive move at Plassey was a bribe, not a charge."),
"d-halley": dict(
 bluf="The comet's predicted return was the most dramatic confirmation of Newton's physics: a theory had forecast an event in the heavens decades in advance. It made Newtonian science the accepted framework across Europe.",
 why="Newton's gravity could, for the first time, predict a comet's orbit.",
 then="The comet was named after Halley, and Newtonian physics won over its last doubters in Europe.",
 now="It returns about every 76 years; it was last seen in 1986 and will be back in 2061.",
 lens="Theories earn trust by making specific predictions that could fail, and then don't."),
"sa-panipat": dict(
 bluf="The Afghan victory at Panipat broke the Marathas, the strongest Indian power of the day, just as the East India Company was taking Bengal. With no Indian power able to unite the north, the British faced weaker, divided opponents.",
 why="Rivalry for control of the decaying Mughal Empire's northern lands.",
 then="Maratha power in the north was broken for a decade, and the Afghans withdrew, leaving a vacuum that gave the East India Company room to expand.",
 now="'Panipat' remains a byword for disaster in Marathi.",
 lens="When two regional powers bleed each other, a third often reaps the benefit."),
"eu176253b": dict(
 bluf="Rousseau argued that legitimate authority comes only from the collective will of the people, not from kings or tradition. His idea of popular sovereignty fuelled the French Revolution, and his 'general will', which can override individuals, has been cited by democrats and authoritarians alike.",
 why="Enlightenment thinkers were debating what makes political power legitimate.",
 then="His ideas of popular sovereignty became watchwords of the French Revolution, especially for the Jacobins.",
 now="Arguments about majority rule versus individual rights still return to Rousseau's 'general will'.",
 lens="Popular sovereignty can justify both democracy and tyranny in the people's name: ask who gets to define 'the will of the people'."),
"eu176253": dict(
 bluf="Catherine II, a German princess who seized power in a coup, expanded Russia to the Black Sea and deep into Poland. She combined Enlightenment rhetoric with autocracy and deepened serfdom, making Russia a dominant European power.",
 why="Peter III had alienated the army and the church; Catherine had built alliances at court and among the guards regiments.",
 then="She annexed Crimea (1783), founded Black Sea ports such as Odessa, took the largest share of partitioned Poland and crushed the Pugachev peasant rebellion (1773-75). Serfdom spread into the newly conquered lands of southern Ukraine.",
 now="Her conquest of Crimea and 'New Russia' in southern Ukraine is invoked by Russian nationalists to justify claims on Ukrainian territory.",
 lens="Enlightened rhetoric and autocratic practice often coexist: judge rulers by the institutions they build, not the philosophers they write to."),
"na176316": dict(
 bluf="France's surrender of almost all of North America left Britain dominant from Hudson Bay to Florida. By removing the French threat, it also removed the colonists' main reason to depend on Britain, setting up the Revolution.",
 why="Britain won the Seven Years' War, and France gave up its North American empire to make peace.",
 then="Free of the French threat, colonists needed British protection much less. Britain's Proclamation of 1763, meant to calm Native nations after Pontiac's War, barred settlement west of the Appalachians and angered colonists.",
 now="Britain's victory set up both Canada's British identity and the American Revolution.",
 lens="Removing a common enemy can loosen the ties within an alliance."),
"na176517": dict(
 bluf="The Stamp Act was Parliament's first direct tax on the American colonies, and united colonial resistance turned a revenue measure into a constitutional crisis. 'No taxation without representation' became the principle that drove the colonies toward independence.",
 why="Britain needed to pay war debt and the cost of keeping troops in America.",
 then="Parliament repealed it a year later but passed the Declaratory Act, asserting its right to legislate for the colonies 'in all cases whatsoever'. Colonists learned that united protest worked, and Britain kept trying new taxes.",
 now="The idea that taxes need the consent of the taxed is now a basic democratic principle.",
 lens="Revenue measures provoke constitutional crises when they raise the question of who has the right to decide."),
"se-ayutthaya": dict(
 bluf="The destruction of Ayutthaya ended Siam's 400-year-old capital and one of Asia's great trading cities. The kingdom was rebuilt by new leaders at Thonburi and then Bangkok, founding the Chakri dynasty that still reigns.",
 why="Burma's expansionist Konbaung kings wanted to crush their old rival.",
 then="A general, Taksin, drove the Burmese out within a year and founded a new capital at Thonburi.",
 now="Ayutthaya's ruins are a UNESCO World Heritage Site, and the city's fall still colours Thai views of Myanmar.",
 lens="The sack of a capital can end one dynasty and make room for a more vigorous one."),
"eu176954": dict(
 bluf="Watt's separate condenser made steam engines efficient enough to power industry anywhere, not just at coal mines. Steam broke the limits of human, animal and water power, the core of the Industrial Revolution, and began large-scale burning of fossil fuels.",
 story="In 1765, repairing a model of Thomas Newcomen's steam engine, James Watt realized it wasted most of its fuel reheating the cylinder. His separate condenser, patented in 1769, cut coal use by about three-quarters. With Matthew Boulton he manufactured engines from 1775 and later adapted them to turn wheels, driving mills and factories.",
 why="Britain had cheap coal, high wages that rewarded labor-saving machines, skilled instrument makers, and patent laws that protected inventors; its mines needed better pumps.",
 then="Rotary steam engines powered textile mills, ironworks and later railways and steamships, freeing factories from rivers. British output and productivity grew, and industrialization spread to Europe and North America.",
 now="Fossil-fuel-powered growth created modern prosperity and began the carbon emissions behind climate change; today's energy transition is an attempt to replace what Watt's engine started.",
 lens="Energy transitions reshape economies and power: the countries that master a new energy source first tend to lead."),
"na177018": dict(
 bluf="The killing of five Bostonians by British soldiers became the Patriots' most powerful propaganda and deepened colonial hostility to British troops. The fair trial that followed also showed the colonists' commitment to the rule of law.",
 why="Tension over British troops stationed in Boston to enforce unpopular customs duties.",
 then="John Adams defended the soldiers in court, and most were acquitted.",
 now="It shows how one image, here Paul Revere's engraving, can shape a political cause.",
 lens="Images and narratives can matter more than facts in political mobilization."),
"oc-cook": dict(
 bluf="Cook's voyage charted Australia's east coast and New Zealand and led directly to British colonization of both. It began the dispossession of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, who were treated as having no legal claim to their land.",
 why="The Royal Society sent Cook to observe the transit of Venus from Tahiti; secret Admiralty orders told him to look for the southern continent.",
 then="Joseph Banks later recommended Botany Bay to Parliament as a site for a penal colony.",
 now="Cook is remembered both as a great navigator and, by many Indigenous people, as the beginning of dispossession.",
 lens="Scientific expeditions often served imperial goals: knowing a coastline was the first step to claiming it."),
"d-cromford": dict(
 bluf="Arkwright's Cromford Mill created the factory system: machines in one building, workers on fixed shifts and wages, and continuous production. It reorganized work and family life and became the model for industrial production worldwide.",
 why="Rising demand for cotton cloth, and a water-powered machine that could spin strong yarn.",
 then="Hundreds of similar mills spread across Britain; Samuel Slater, a former Arkwright apprentice, carried the design to the United States in 1790.",
 now="Cromford is part of the Derwent Valley Mills UNESCO World Heritage Site.",
 lens="The biggest economic changes are often in how work is organized, not just in the machines."),
"eu177255": dict(
 bluf="Russia, Prussia and Austria began dismembering Poland-Lithuania, once one of Europe's largest states, without fighting a war. By 1795 Poland had vanished, showing how a weak state between stronger neighbours could be erased by agreement among them.",
 why="Poland-Lithuania's weak elected kings and paralysed parliament, where any one member could veto legislation, left it unable to reform or resist.",
 then="Poland's reforming constitution of 1791 provoked the second and third partitions (1793, 1795), and Poland did not exist as a state for 123 years.",
 now="Poles still remember being squeezed between Russia and Germany, which shapes Poland's strong support for NATO and Ukraine.",
 lens="A weak state between great powers is in greatest danger when those powers agree to divide it rather than compete for it."),
"na177319": dict(
 bluf="The destruction of the East India Company's tea was a direct challenge to Parliament's authority. Britain's punitive response, the Coercive Acts, united the colonies against it and led directly to war.",
 why="Protest against the Tea Act, which gave the East India Company a monopoly in the colonies and reasserted Parliament's right to tax them.",
 then="Britain closed Boston's port and took over Massachusetts' government, pushing the colonies to unite in the First Continental Congress (1774).",
 now="It remains the go-to symbol of American protest against taxes and distant power.",
 lens="Harsh punishment of one protest can unite many others: Britain's crackdown turned a Boston quarrel into a continental cause."),
"jp-kaitai": dict(
 bluf="The translation of a Dutch anatomy book showed Japanese doctors that Western empirical medicine was more accurate than traditional Chinese models. It turned 'Dutch learning' into a serious scientific movement and laid groundwork for Japan's later modernization.",
 lens="Seeing evidence first-hand changes minds faster than argument."),
"na177520": dict(
 bluf="The first shots of the Revolutionary War turned a political dispute into armed rebellion. Colonial militia proved they would fight, and within weeks New England had Boston under siege and Congress had created an army.",
 why="British troops tried to seize colonial weapons and powder stored at Concord.",
 then="Within weeks colonial forces had Boston under siege, and the Continental Army was formed under Washington.",
 now="April 19 is still Patriots' Day in Massachusetts, the day of the Boston Marathon.",
 lens="Attempts to disarm a population can trigger the uprising they aim to prevent."),
"d-bunker": dict(
 bluf="Bunker Hill showed that colonial militia could inflict heavy losses on British regulars. The British victory was so costly that it made them cautious and gave the Americans confidence.",
 lens="A costly victory can hurt the winner more than defeat hurts the loser."),
"eu177656": dict(
 bluf="Adam Smith argued that wealth comes from productive labour, specialization and free exchange, not from hoarding gold. His case against monopolies and trade barriers founded modern economics and became the intellectual basis of free-market capitalism.",
 story="Smith argued that dividing labour into specialized tasks multiplies productivity, and that when individuals pursue their own interest in competitive markets, an 'invisible hand' tends to benefit society. He attacked mercantilism, monopolies such as the East India Company and trade barriers, while insisting that governments must provide defence, justice and public works.",
 why="Smith set out to explain how nations grow rich, and to refute mercantilism, the doctrine that wealth means hoarding gold through trade surpluses.",
 then="Britain moved toward free trade in the 1800s, culminating in the repeal of the Corn Laws (1846). Smith shaped classical economists such as Ricardo and Malthus, and critics such as Marx.",
 now="Modern economics starts here. Today's renewed fights over tariffs, industrial policy and free trade are still argued in Smith's terms.",
 lens="Ask whether a policy creates wealth or merely moves it around; Smith's question still cuts through economic arguments."),
"na177621": dict(
 bluf="The Declaration justified the break with Britain by asserting universal rights and government by consent, which made the Revolution a cause for the world rather than a colonial dispute. Its claim that 'all men are created equal' clashed from the start with slavery, a contradiction that has driven American politics ever since.",
 story="Fifteen months into the war, on July 4, 1776, the Continental Congress adopted Thomas Jefferson's draft announcing independence. It argued that governments get their power from the consent of the governed and that people may overthrow tyrants, then listed grievances against George III. Jefferson enslaved hundreds of people over his life, and a passage condemning the slave trade was cut from his draft.",
 why="War had begun, and independence was needed to win foreign allies, especially France, which would not back rebels seeking only better terms.",
 then="France saw a real cause worth backing and joined the war in 1778.",
 now="Its words inspired revolutions worldwide and were used by abolitionists, suffragists and civil rights leaders to demand that America live up to them.",
 lens="Revolutionary principles often outrun the interests of those who wrote them, and later groups claim them."),
"d-commonsense": dict(
 bluf="Common Sense turned colonial resistance into a demand for independence and a republic, in language ordinary people could read. It showed the political power of mass print persuasion.",
 lens="Ideas spread fastest when written in plain language for ordinary readers."),
"d-saratoga": dict(
 bluf="The surrender of an entire British army at Saratoga persuaded France to enter the war, turning a colonial rebellion into a global conflict Britain could not win. It was the strategic turning point of the Revolution.",
 why="Britain planned to cut New England off from the other colonies along the Hudson, but poor coordination between British commanders left Burgoyne without support.",
 lens="Battles matter most for how they change others' calculations: Saratoga's real result came in Paris."),
"d-france1778": dict(
 bluf="The French alliance gave the Americans money, troops and above all a navy, making victory possible. For France, revenge on Britain came at the price of debts that helped bring on its own revolution.",
 lens="Great powers back rebels to weaken their rivals, and the cost can come home in unexpected ways."),
"la178011": dict(
 bluf="Túpac Amaru II's rebellion was the largest Indigenous uprising in colonial Spanish America, a revolt against abusive officials and forced labour. Its brutal suppression left Creole elites afraid of Indigenous mobilization, which shaped Peru's cautious, elite-led road to independence.",
 why="Crushing taxes, forced purchases of goods from officials, and the mita forced-labour system.",
 then="He was captured and executed in 1781, and Spain banned Inca dress, symbols and titles. The rebellion continued under Túpac Katari in Upper Peru (Bolivia).",
 now="He is a national hero in Peru and an icon of resistance across Latin America.",
 lens="Fear of mass revolt from below makes elites cautious revolutionaries."),
"na178122": dict(
 bluf="Yorktown, won by a combined American-French army and the French navy, effectively ended the war. Sea power decided it: Britain could not supply or rescue an army it could not protect at sea.",
 why="A French fleet under de Grasse blocked the Chesapeake, trapping Cornwallis's army against the sea.",
 then="Britain lost the will to fight and opened peace talks.",
 now="American independence was won with decisive French help, a fact often forgotten.",
 lens="Control of the sea decides wars fought along coastlines."),
"se-chakri": dict(
 bluf="The Chakri dynasty rebuilt Siam around a new capital at Bangkok and made it the dominant power of mainland Southeast Asia. Its kings later kept Siam independent of European empires by modernizing and balancing Britain against France.",
 why="To rebuild the kingdom after Ayutthaya's fall, at a more defensible site.",
 then="Siam became the strongest power in mainland Southeast Asia, extending control over parts of Laos, Cambodia and Malaya.",
 now="Thailand's king is still a Chakri, and the monarchy remains politically central, protected by strict lèse-majesté laws.",
 lens="Small states survive between empires by modernizing and playing rivals off each other."),
"na178323": dict(
 bluf="Britain's recognition of American independence created the first modern state born from a colonial revolt. The generous border at the Mississippi gave the United States room to expand west, at the expense of Native nations who were not consulted.",
 why="Britain wanted to end a costly war, and also to pull the United States away from France with generous terms.",
 then="The new nation was free but weak, deeply in debt and loosely governed.",
 now="The treaty roughly doubled the area of the former colonies and opened the way west.",
 lens="A winner may offer a rival generous terms to pull it away from a bigger enemy."),
"d-shays": dict(
 bluf="Shays' Rebellion convinced many American leaders that the Articles of Confederation were too weak to keep order or pay debts. It helped push the states toward the Constitutional Convention and a stronger national government.",
 lens="Domestic unrest often triggers state-building: elites centralize power when they fear disorder."),
"na178724": dict(
 bluf="The Constitution created a federal republic with separated powers, checks and balances, and a national government able to tax and govern. It is the oldest written national constitution still in force, and its compromises, including over slavery, shaped American politics for centuries.",
 story="The Articles of Confederation left Congress unable to tax, regulate trade or enforce laws. In 1787 delegates in Philadelphia wrote a new framework: a two-chamber Congress (the 'Great Compromise' between large and small states), an elected president, an independent judiciary and federal supremacy over the states. To keep the South in, they counted enslaved people as three-fifths of a person for representation and protected the slave trade until 1808.",
 why="The national government could not tax, pay its debts or keep order.",
 then="After a fierce ratification fight that produced the Federalist Papers, it took effect in 1789, and the Bill of Rights followed in 1791.",
 now="It is the oldest written national constitution still in use. Debates over the Electoral College, the Senate's small-state advantage and federal versus state power are rooted in its compromises.",
 lens="Institutions built by compromise carry those compromises for generations: read a constitution for what it was trying to balance."),
"d-abolsociety": dict(
 bluf="The London abolition society invented the modern pressure campaign, using evidence, mass petitions, consumer boycotts and visual propaganda to turn public opinion against the slave trade. Within 20 years it had won abolition of the British trade.",
 lens="Movements win by making a distant injustice visible and personal to ordinary people."),
"oc-firstfleet": dict(
 bluf="The First Fleet founded British Australia as a penal colony on Aboriginal land that British law treated as belonging to no one. It began the dispossession of Indigenous Australians and the building of the settler society that became modern Australia.",
 why="After American independence, Britain could no longer ship convicts to America and needed somewhere new; the colony also gave Britain a foothold in the Pacific.",
 then="Settlement spread across the continent, and frontier violence and disease devastated Aboriginal societies. About 162,000 convicts were transported by 1868.",
 now="January 26 is Australia Day, and to many Aboriginal Australians 'Invasion Day'. The legal doctrine that the land had belonged to no one was overturned only by the Mabo decision in 1992.",
 lens="Legal fictions about 'empty land' justify settler colonization, and reversing them reshapes politics centuries later."),
"eu178957": dict(
 bluf="The French Revolution destroyed absolute monarchy and aristocratic privilege in Europe's most powerful country and proclaimed universal rights, popular sovereignty and the nation. Its ideals and its violence, from the Terror to revolutionary war, defined modern politics.",
 story="With the state bankrupt and bread prices soaring, Louis XVI called the Estates-General in 1789. The Third Estate declared itself a National Assembly, Parisians stormed the Bastille on July 14, and the Assembly abolished feudal privileges and issued the Declaration of the Rights of Man. Over the next decade France became a republic, executed the king, fought most of Europe and lived through the Terror.",
 why="A state bankrupted by wars, including its aid to the Americans, that could not tax its privileged nobles and clergy; an educated middle class shut out of power; Enlightenment ideas; and failed harvests in 1788.",
 trig="Louis XVI summoned the Estates-General to approve new taxes, during the bread crisis of 1788-89.",
 then="France abolished feudalism, seized church lands, became a republic and executed Louis XVI (1793). War with Europe's monarchies and internal revolts brought the Terror (1793-94). Napoleon took power in 1799 and spread revolutionary legal reforms across Europe.",
 now="Liberty, equality and nationalism, the language of 'left' and 'right', declarations of human rights, the secular state, and the argument over whether revolutions must turn violent all trace back here.",
 lens="Revolutions often start with a fiscal crisis: when a state cannot pay its bills and the privileged will not pay taxes, the old order is at risk."),
"d-washington": dict(
 bluf="Washington's presidency turned the Constitution into a working government and set precedents (a cabinet, two terms, a peaceful handover) that the text did not require. His voluntary departure after two terms established that power in the republic would rotate.",
 lens="The first holder of an office defines it: founders set norms as much as rules."),
"d-rightsofman": dict(
 bluf="The Declaration set out natural, universal rights and the sovereignty of the nation, replacing privilege and divine-right monarchy as the basis of French law. It became a model for human rights declarations worldwide, while its exclusion of women and the enslaved showed the limits of 'universal' rights.",
 lens="Universal declarations invite excluded groups to claim them."),
"d-womensmarch": dict(
 bluf="The women's march forced the royal family to move to Paris, putting the king under the control of the revolutionary crowd. It showed the power of popular mobilization, and of bread prices, in driving the Revolution.",
 lens="Food prices are political: when bread is scarce, governments fall."),
"la179112": dict(
 bluf="The Haitian Revolution was the only slave revolt in history to found a nation, and it destroyed the world's most profitable colony. It proved enslaved people could defeat European armies, terrified slaveholding societies, and pushed Napoleon to sell Louisiana to the United States.",
 story="Saint-Domingue produced much of the world's sugar and coffee using the labor of about 500,000 enslaved Africans, worked so hard that planters constantly imported more. In August 1791 they rose on the northern plain. Led by Toussaint Louverture and later Jean-Jacques Dessalines, they fought off Spanish and British invasions and, in 1802-03, Napoleon's army.",
 why="Brutal sugar slavery; free people of colour demanding equal rights; and the French Revolution's declarations of liberty, which divided the colony's whites and inspired the enslaved.",
 trig="A ceremony at Bois Caïman and coordinated uprisings across the northern plain in August 1791.",
 then="France abolished slavery in 1794, and Haiti declared independence in 1804. Napoleon's defeat there led him to sell Louisiana (1803). Haiti was isolated: the United States refused recognition until 1862, and France demanded a huge indemnity in 1825 in return for recognizing it.",
 now="Haiti paid the indemnity and related loans until 1947, draining its economy. The revolution is a touchstone for Black freedom and anti-colonial movements.",
 lens="A revolution in one place can redraw maps elsewhere: Haiti's victory is one reason the United States doubled in size."),
}
